def E(d,disp,prec,eye,place,head,dig,quote="",conf="verified",src=""):
    return dict(date_iso=d,date_display=disp,precision=prec,eye=eye,place=place,headline=head,digest=dig,quote=quote,confidence=conf,source_note=src)
N="Palace of Justice, Nuremberg"
entries=[
E("1945-06-07","6–7 June 1945","range","jackson","Washington","Report to Truman: the case must rest on evidence, not vengeance",
 "Jackson's report to the President, released by the White House, argued that the leaders should be tried rather than shot out of hand, and that the case should be built mainly from the regime's own captured documents. He proposed charging the waging of aggressive war as a crime in itself.",
 "","approx","Report to the President by Mr. Justice Jackson, June 6, 1945 (State Department Bulletin, 10 June 1945); release date as commonly cited."),
E("1945-08-08","Wednesday 8 August 1945","day","imt","London","Four powers sign the Charter that creates the court",
 "The US, UK, USSR and France signed the London Agreement and its annexed Charter, setting up an International Military Tribunal of four judges. Article 6 defined crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity, and the Charter ruled out official position or superior orders as a complete defence.",
 "","verified","London Agreement and Charter, 8 Aug 1945 (Blue Series vol. 1)."),
E("1945-10-18","Thursday 18 October 1945","day","imt","Allied Control Council building, Berlin","The indictment is lodged in Berlin",
 "At its only session in Berlin, with the Soviet judge Nikitchenko presiding, the Tribunal received the indictment against 24 individuals and six organisations on four counts: common plan or conspiracy, crimes against peace, war crimes, and crimes against humanity. The trial was set to open in Nuremberg.",
 "","verified","Blue Series vol. 1 (Berlin session)."),
E("1945-10-19","Friday 19 October 1945","day","neave","Nuremberg jail","Neave carries the indictment from cell to cell",
 "Major Neave served each prisoner with a German copy of the indictment, the Charter and a list of German lawyers, and told each of his right to counsel. His certificate lists twenty men served at Nuremberg that day, Ley and Hess among them. In his much later book he remembered Göring as composed and contemptuous of the charges' legal basis, Hess as absorbed in a novel, and Ribbentrop as anxious about finding a lawyer.",
 "","verified","Certificate of service dated 24 Oct 1945 (Avalon v1-06); impressions from Neave, 'The day of the indictment', Spectator 21 Feb 1976 (memoir, written later)."),
E("1945-10-22","Monday 22 October 1945","day","dodd","Nuremberg","A sudden promotion in the staff shake-up",
 "Dodd told Grace it had been an unusual day: around six in the evening Colonel Storey showed him Jackson's order reorganising the American staff, placing Dodd among four men with full authority over the work. He was astonished.",
 "","verified","Letters from Nuremberg, 22 Oct 1945, via UConn blog 'A Rather Unusual Day' (28 Oct 2015)."),
E("1945-10-25","late October 1945","range","gilbert","Nuremberg jail","The defendants write their verdicts on the indictment",
 "Newly arrived, Gilbert asked each defendant to write a line on his copy of the indictment. Most wrote that they were personally innocent and blamed Hitler or Himmler, or dismissed the trial as victors' justice; Streicher and Rosenberg blamed the Jews. Robert Ley hanged himself in his cell that week, before the trial opened.",
 "","approx","Nuremberg Diary (1947), opening section; exact day of each comment not established here."),
E("1945-11-17","Saturday 17 November 1945","day","dodd","Nuremberg","'Not ready' three days before the opening",
 "Dodd admitted that the prosecution was not ready for the 20 November start, and hoped they could improvise and still make a good showing. Two days earlier he had tested the new simultaneous-interpretation system, where a listener chose English, French, Russian or German with a dial.",
 "","verified","Letters from Nuremberg, 15 and 17 Nov 1945, via UConn blog 'At Long Last it Begins' (2 Dec 2015)."),
E("1945-11-20","Tuesday 20 November 1945","day","imt",N,"First day: twenty men in the dock, the indictment read aloud",
 "Lord Justice Lawrence opened the trial. Of the 24 indicted, Ley was dead, Krupp was too ill to stand trial and Bormann was tried in his absence, so twenty-one sat in the dock. Prosecutors of the four nations took turns reading the indictment through the day.",
 "","verified","Blue Series vol. 2, first day."),
E("1945-11-21","Wednesday 21 November 1945","day","goering",N,"Göring pleads, and is stopped from making a speech",
 "Called first, Göring tried to read a statement before pleading and was told by the President that defendants could only plead. He then declared himself not guilty in the sense of the indictment; every other defendant followed with a not-guilty plea, some in their own phrasing.",
 "I declare myself in the sense of the Indictment not guilty.","verified","Blue Series vol. 2, second day, pleas."),
E("1945-11-21","Wednesday 21 November 1945","day","jackson",N,"Jackson's opening: law in place of vengeance",
 "Jackson spoke for most of the day. He said the crimes were so calculated and destructive that civilisation could not ignore them, and that the victors were choosing to try their enemies rather than punish them unheard. He promised to prove the case from the defendants' own documents and warned that the court would be judged by history.",
 "stay the hand of vengeance","verified","Opening statement, 21 Nov 1945 (Blue Series vol. 2; Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression vol. 1, GPO 1946)."),
E("1945-11-29","Thursday 29 November 1945","day","imt",N,"The court watches film of the liberated camps",
 "The American prosecution showed 'Nazi Concentration Camps', an hour of footage shot by Allied army cameramen as the camps were liberated. Lights were left on in the dock so the defendants' faces could be watched. The record notes the film's showing and its affidavits of authenticity; the court rose without further business.",
 "","verified","Blue Series vol. 2, 29 Nov 1945 (document 2430-PS)."),
E("1945-11-29","evening, 29 November 1945","day","gilbert","Nuremberg jail","Reactions in the dock and the cells",
 "Gilbert and the psychiatrist Douglas Kelley watched the defendants during the film. He noted several men turning away or in tears and Schacht refusing to look; in the cells that evening some said they were ashamed, while Göring complained that the film had spoiled a good day in court for him.",
 "","approx","Nuremberg Diary (1947), entry for the film; wording of individual remarks is Gilbert's."),
E("1945-11-30","Friday 30 November 1945","day","imt",N,"Lahousen testifies; Hess says his amnesia was a pretence",
 "General Erwin Lahousen of the Abwehr, the first prosecution witness, described orders he had heard for operations in Poland and against Soviet prisoners. In the afternoon, during argument over his fitness to stand trial, Hess rose and announced that his memory had returned and that he had feigned its loss for tactical reasons. The Tribunal later ruled him fit.",
 "","verified","Blue Series vol. 2, 30 Nov 1945."),
E("1945-12-04","Tuesday 4 December 1945","day","shawcross",N,"The British case: treaties broken, wars of aggression",
 "Shawcross opened the British case on crimes against peace. He set out the treaties Germany had signed and broken and argued that by 1939 aggressive war was already outlawed, so the court was applying existing law rather than inventing it.",
 "","verified","Opening statement, 4 Dec 1945 (Blue Series vol. 3)."),
E("1945-12-06","Thursday 6 December 1945","day","dodd","Nuremberg","Praise for Shawcross, impatience with his own side",
 "Dodd thought Shawcross had given a lawyer-like opening that was well received. He was less happy with his American colleague Sidney Alderman, who he felt dragged on for hours, and he hoped for a Christmas recess long enough to get home to Connecticut.",
 "","verified","Letters from Nuremberg, 6 Dec 1945, via UConn blog 'Presentation of the Cases' (9 Dec 2015)."),
E("1946-01-03","Thursday 3 January 1946","day","imt",N,"Ohlendorf describes the mobile killing units",
 "Otto Ohlendorf, who had led Einsatzgruppe D from June 1941 to June 1942, was examined by Colonel John Harlan Amen. He testified calmly that his units had reported about 90,000 people killed in that year, including women and children, and that the orders had come orally before the invasion of the Soviet Union.",
 "","verified","Avalon 01-03-46 (26th day)."),
E("1946-01-17","Thursday 17 January 1946","day","imt",N,"France opens: de Menthon on crimes against the human person",
 "The French chief prosecutor François de Menthon, a former Resistance leader, opened the French case, describing Nazism as an attack on the very idea of the human person and setting out the occupation of western Europe, forced labour and deportations.",
 "","verified","Blue Series vol. 5, 17 Jan 1946."),
E("1946-01-28","Monday 28 January 1946","day","vaillant",N,"A survivor of Auschwitz takes the stand",
 "Examined by the French prosecutor Charles Dubost, Vaillant-Couturier described her arrest in 1942, her deportation in January 1943 in a convoy of about 230 French women of whom 49 returned, and her time in Birkenau and Ravensbrück. Cross-examined for the SS by Dr Hans Marx about how she had survived and what Germans could have known, she answered that she had repeated only what she had seen or checked with several witnesses.",
 "I have never quoted anything which has not previously been verified","verified","Avalon 01-28-46 (44th day)."),
E("1946-02-08","Friday 8 February 1946","day","rudenko",N,"The Soviet case opens",
 "Rudenko opened for the USSR, describing the invasion of 22 June 1941 as premeditated aggression and setting out the destruction in the occupied Soviet territories, the treatment of prisoners of war and the killing of civilians.",
 "","verified","Blue Series vol. 7, 8 Feb 1946."),
E("1946-02-11","Monday 11 February 1946","day","rudenko",N,"A surprise witness: Field Marshal Paulus",
 "Rudenko's team produced Friedrich Paulus, who had surrendered at Stalingrad and been held in Soviet captivity. Paulus testified that the attack on the USSR had been planned well in advance, naming Keitel and Jodl among those responsible; the defendants, who had not known he was in Nuremberg, were visibly shaken.",
 "","approx","Blue Series vol. 7, 11–12 Feb 1946."),
E("1946-03-13","Wednesday 13 March 1946","day","goering",N,"Göring begins his own defence",
 "Questioned by his counsel Otto Stahmer, Göring gave a long, fluent account of his career and the regime, defending the seizure of power, rearmament and the Four-Year Plan, and claiming credit rather than denying it. His testimony ran over several days and impressed even hostile observers with its command of detail.",
 "","approx","Blue Series vol. 9, from 13 Mar 1946."),
E("1946-03-18","18–20 March 1946","range","jackson",N,"Jackson cross-examines Göring",
 "Jackson's cross-examination began on the afternoon of 18 March and ran to 20 March. He lost several early exchanges over documents and translations and protested to the bench that Göring was making speeches instead of answering; on 20 March he took Göring through the anti-Jewish decrees from 1935 to 1941, the November 1938 pogrom and the seizure of art.",
 "","verified","Avalon 03-20-46 (86th day); Robert H. Jackson Center."),
E("1946-03-20","Wednesday 20 March 1946","day","dodd","Nuremberg","Jackson 'in good form' against a tired Göring",
 "Dodd wrote that Jackson's cross-examination had taken the whole day, that Jackson had done well, and that Göring looked ill. His letter is a loyal counterweight to the press verdict that Jackson had been beaten.",
 "","verified","Letters from Nuremberg, p. 267, via UConn blog 'Göring on the stand' (23 Mar 2016)."),
E("1946-03-21","Thursday 21 March 1946","day","maxwell-fyfe","Nuremberg","Göring is 'extremely clever', but knocked off his perch",
 "Maxwell Fyfe told his wife that Göring had been very clever, calm and factual, though a little dull, and that his own cross-examination, built on the shooting of the fifty RAF officers recaptured after the Stalag Luft III escape, had gone off all right and knocked Göring reasonably off his perch.",
 "","approx","Letter to Sylvia Maxwell Fyfe, quoted in The Spectator (2025); Kilmuir Papers."),
E("1946-03-23","Saturday 23 March 1946","day","maxwell-fyfe","Nuremberg","'At last finished with Göring'",
 "He wrote that they had at last finished with Göring, after Soviet and French cross-examination, and that Hess's case would follow on the Monday.",
 "","approx","Letter quoted in The Spectator (2025)."),
E("1946-03-26","Tuesday 26 March 1946","day","dodd","Nuremberg","Grumbling at Amen and at Judge Biddle",
 "Dodd complained that Colonel Amen had wasted two hours on repetitive questions to win publicity, and that the American judge Francis Biddle had done more than anyone to slow the trial, his remarks taking up the rest of the day.",
 "","verified","Letters from Nuremberg, pp. 271–272, via UConn blog."),
E("1946-04-05","Friday 5 April 1946","day","hoess","Nuremberg jail","Höss signs an affidavit",
 "In an affidavit taken in Nuremberg, Höss set out his service as commandant of Auschwitz and gave his estimate of the number of people killed there, about three million in all including those who died of hunger and disease. Later scholarship puts the number killed at Auschwitz at about 1.1 million; Höss's figures were his own.",
 "","verified","Document 3868-PS (USA-819), per Avalon 04-15-46."),
E("1946-04-10","about 9–11 April 1946","range","gilbert","Nuremberg jail","Gilbert interviews the former commandant",
 "Gilbert spoke with Höss in his cell several times. He recorded a man who described mass killing as a technical assignment, said he had never questioned Himmler's orders, and showed no emotion; Gilbert used the word apathetic to sum him up.",
 "","approx","Nuremberg Diary (1947), April 1946 entries on Höss; exact days not checked here."),
E("1946-04-15","Monday 15 April 1946","day","hoess",N,"Called for the defence, Höss confirms the affidavit",
 "Höss was called by Kaltenbrunner's counsel, Dr Kurt Kauffmann, to show that Kaltenbrunner had no authority over the camps. Instead, examined and then cross-examined by Colonel Amen, he confirmed his affidavit, testified that he had commanded Auschwitz from May 1940 to December 1943, and said that Himmler had told him in 1941 of Hitler's order and demanded strict secrecy.",
 "the Fuehrer had given the order for a final solution","verified","Avalon 04-15-46 (108th day)."),
E("1946-04-18","evening, 18 April 1946","day","gilbert","Göring's cell, Nuremberg jail","Göring on how nations are led to war",
 "Sweating in his cell, Göring told Gilbert that ordinary people never want war, but that leaders can always bring them along, whatever the system, by telling them they are under attack and condemning pacifists as unpatriotic. Gilbert objected that in a democracy the people have a say through their representatives; Göring was unmoved.",
 "","verified","Nuremberg Diary (1947), 18 Apr 1946; the remark is omitted from the 1948 London edition."),
E("1946-06-20","19–20 June 1946","range","speer",N,"Speer accepts a share of the leadership's responsibility",
 "Examined by his counsel Hans Flächsner, Speer described his work as armaments minister and his use of forced labour, and said that a leader must bear responsibility for the regime's catastrophe as a whole, even for what he did not know. He also described his opposition to Hitler's scorched-earth orders in 1945.",
 "","approx","Blue Series vol. 16, 19–20 Jun 1946."),
E("1946-06-21","Friday 21 June 1946","day","jackson",N,"Jackson cross-examines Speer",
 "Jackson pressed Speer on the forced-labour programme and conditions in the armaments factories, but the exchange was courteous compared with the Göring cross-examination; Speer kept to his line of general responsibility without personal knowledge of the killings.",
 "","approx","Blue Series vol. 16, 21 Jun 1946."),
E("1946-07-16","Tuesday 16 July 1946","day","dodd","Nuremberg","Drafting the Justice's final speech; the defendants look doomed",
 "Dodd apologised for not writing: he had been working hard on Jackson's closing speech while the defence lawyers delivered their final arguments. He noticed that the defendants seemed to sense the end coming: Göring was grey and dejected, and Keitel already wore what he called the mask of the doomed.",
 "","verified","Letters from Nuremberg, pp. 335–336, via UConn blog 'Mask of the doomed' (20 Jul 2016)."),
E("1946-07-26","Friday 26 July 1946","day","jackson",N,"Jackson's closing: no innocence without denying the war",
 "In his summation Jackson took each defendant's claim of ignorance in turn and set it against the documents. He ended by saying that a verdict of not guilty would be as true as saying there had been no war, no dead and no crime.",
 "as true to say that there has been no war","verified","Closing address, 26 Jul 1946 (Blue Series vol. 19)."),
E("1946-07-27","26–27 July 1946","range","shawcross",N,"Shawcross's closing: the individual behind the numbers",
 "Shawcross argued that each defendant shared in the murders as a member of the leadership. To make the scale concrete he read from the affidavit of the German engineer Hermann Gräbe about a mass shooting near Dubno in Ukraine, which he had witnessed in 1942, and asked the court to see the victims as people rather than statistics.",
 "","approx","Closing speech (Blue Series vol. 19, 26–27 Jul 1946)."),
E("1946-08-31","Saturday 31 August 1946","day","goering",N,"Final statement: a denial to the end",
 "In the defendants' final statements Göring denied that he had ordered or known of the mass killings, condemned them, and said he had never wanted war; he called the trial the victors' judgment and said the German people should not be blamed.",
 "","approx","Blue Series vol. 22, 31 Aug 1946."),
E("1946-08-31","Saturday 31 August 1946","day","speer",N,"Speer's final statement: a warning about technology",
 "Speer spoke of the regime as the first dictatorship to use modern technology to dominate its people, and warned that a future war fought with atomic weapons could destroy civilisation. The court then adjourned to consider its judgment for a month.",
 "","approx","Blue Series vol. 22, 31 Aug 1946."),
E("1946-09-07","issue of 7 September 1946","day","west","Nuremberg (written there in summer 1946)","'Extraordinary Exile': a court sunk in tedium",
 "West described a courtroom where everyone, from the judges to the guards, was weary after ten months, and a town of ruins around it. She drew pitiless sketches of the defendants, including Göring as soft and theatrical, and of the occupation community marooned in Nuremberg.",
 "","approx","The New Yorker, 7 Sep 1946; later in A Train of Powder (1955)."),
E("1946-09-19","Thursday 19 September 1946","day","dodd","Cannes","On leave on the Riviera as the verdict is delayed",
 "Writing from Cannes, Dodd described the beaches, Nice and the Monte Carlo casino, then reported that the judgment had been postponed a week; he was disappointed, given how long the trial had already run.",
 "","verified","Letters from Nuremberg, 19 Sep 1946, via UConn blog 'The end draws near' (28 Sep 2016)."),
E("1946-09-26","Thursday 26 September 1946","day","dodd","Nuremberg","His last full letter from Germany",
 "Back in Nuremberg, Dodd called this his last full letter: the verdict was expected the coming Monday and Tuesday, Jackson had thanked him warmly for his work, and he planned to leave as soon as the sentences were read and sail home from Bremerhaven.",
 "","verified","Letters from Nuremberg, 26 Sep 1946, via UConn blog."),
E("1946-09-30","Monday 30 September 1946","day","imt",N,"The judgment is read: law, organisations, the conspiracy",
 "The judges took turns reading the judgment. It held that aggressive war was the supreme international crime, found the SS, SD, Gestapo and the Nazi Leadership Corps to be criminal organisations, but declined to so declare the SA, the Reich Cabinet and the General Staff and High Command. It narrowed the conspiracy count to planning aggressive war.",
 "","verified","Judgment, 30 Sep – 1 Oct 1946 (Blue Series vol. 1 and 22)."),
E("1946-10-01","Tuesday 1 October 1946","day","imt",N,"Verdicts and sentences; the Soviet judge dissents",
 "Nineteen defendants were convicted and three, Schacht, von Papen and Fritzsche, acquitted. In the afternoon each convicted man was brought up alone to hear his sentence: twelve to death by hanging (Bormann in his absence), three to life imprisonment, four to terms of ten to twenty years. The Soviet judge Iona Nikitchenko filed a dissent against the acquittals, Hess's life sentence and the failure to condemn the Reich Cabinet and General Staff.",
 "","verified","Blue Series vol. 22, 1 Oct 1946."),
E("1946-10-01","Tuesday 1 October 1946","day","speer",N,"Twenty years",
 "Speer, convicted on counts three and four for the forced-labour programme, was sentenced to twenty years. The judgment noted in mitigation that he had opposed Hitler's destruction orders at the end of the war. He served the full term in Spandau.",
 "","verified","Blue Series vol. 22, 1 Oct 1946."),
E("1946-10-01","afternoon, 1 October 1946","day","gilbert","Nuremberg jail","Visiting each man after his sentence",
 "Gilbert went down the cell block as the men returned from the courtroom. He recorded Göring pale and asking to be left alone after saying the single word of his sentence, Speer saying twenty years was fair enough, and Fritzsche, acquitted, scarcely believing it.",
 "","approx","Nuremberg Diary (1947), final entries."),
E("1946-10-01","30 Sep – 1 Oct 1946","range","west",N,"The last two days in court",
 "West was in the press gallery for the judgment. She recorded the long reading, the ban on photographing the men as they heard their sentences, and the strangeness of the condemned walking out one by one through the sliding door at the back of the dock.",
 "","verified","'The Birch Leaves Falling', New Yorker 26 Oct 1946 (NJ curriculum reprint)."),
E("1946-10-07","7 October 1946","day","jackson","Washington","Final report to the President",
 "Jackson's final report to Truman claimed the trial had made aggressive war a crime under international law and had documented the regime's record from its own files, for the use of historians. He had resigned his post as chief of counsel and returned to the Supreme Court.",
 "","approx","Report to the President, 7 Oct 1946 (US State Dept., Report of Robert H. Jackson, 1949)."),
E("1946-10-15","Tuesday 15 October 1946","day","goering","Cell 5, Nuremberg jail","Göring takes poison before the hangings",
 "Late in the evening, about two hours before he was due to be hanged, Göring killed himself with a cyanide capsule he had kept hidden. He left several letters; he had earlier asked in vain to be shot rather than hanged. How he had kept the poison hidden was never settled.",
 "","verified","Kingsbury Smith INS report, 16 Oct 1946 (timing); details of the letters from later accounts."),
E("1946-10-16","Wednesday 16 October 1946","day","ksmith","Gymnasium, Nuremberg jail","Ten men hanged before dawn",
 "Smith reported that the ten condemned men were hanged one after another on gallows built in the prison gymnasium, beginning with Ribbentrop shortly after 1 a.m. and ending with Seyss-Inquart at about 2.45 a.m., before some thirty witnesses including eight correspondents. Göring's body was then brought in and shown to the witnesses.",
 "","verified","INS pool report, 16 Oct 1946 (Famous Trials reprint)."),
E("1946-10-19","dateline 19 October 1946","day","west","Nuremberg and Berlin","'The Birch Leaves Falling': the news of Göring",
 "West wrote of hearing about Göring's suicide and how it unsettled her sense of what the year-long trial had meant, and of the ruined statues and streets of Berlin seen on the journey. She set the condemned men beside William Joyce, hanged in London that January for treason, whose trial she had also reported.",
 "","verified","New Yorker, published 26 Oct 1946 (NJ curriculum reprint)."),
]

